const rawSourceContent = "Key Takeaways\nUnderstanding Stress & Chronic Stress\nStress and the Mind-Body Connection\nCommon Signs & Symptoms of Stress\nCauses of Chronic Stress in Women\nStress vs Anxiety Explained\nImpact of Stress on Daily Life\nSupporting Healthy Mind-Body Balance\nHealthy Stress Management Strategies\nNeuro-Ayurveda Stress Management Approach\nEmotional Resilience & Long-Term Wellbeing\nManovaidya Assessment & Support Process\nWhen Professional Guidance Is Needed\nFAQs About Stress & Mind-Body Balance\n\nStress & Anxiety\n\nStress Management & Mind-Body Balance Treatment for Women in India\n\nLearn about Stress, Tension & the Mind-Body Connection for emotional wellness.\n\nStress is an inevitable by-product of life. Stress comes in many forms and for different women, different periods in their lives. Stress can help us to cope with challenges in small quantities. Stress, however, can start to impact on emotional and physical well-being when it occurs too often or lasts too long. [1]\n\nFor many women, the roles of career, family, parenting, and caring for others, along with personal expectations overlap. This ongoing pressure can lead to emotional tension over time which can interfere with sleep, focus, energy, and quality of life. [1]\n\nStressed doesn't just impact the mind. It also has an impact on the body. During stressful time, some women experience headache, muscle tension, digestive pain, tired or disturbed sleep. Some people may feel anxious or irritated, or be unable to relax or unwind. [1][3]\n\nThe mind-body connection is a close connection between emotional wellbeing and physical health. Physical health issues can have an emotional impact and vice versa. [3]\n\nSometimes it is OK to be stressed. If stress is chronic, overwhelming or starts to affect work, relationships, sleep or day-to-day functioning, however, then it may be time to get professional help. [1][2]\n\nWe at Manovaidya believe that emotional wellbeing is cultivated through the equilibrium of brain health, physical health, healthy lifestyle choices, good sleep and emotional resilience skills. We don't just look at the symptoms that may present themselves, but we want to understand the factors that are causing emotional and physical imbalance.\n\nUnder the guidance of Dr. Ankush Garg (Autism, ADHD, Child Development & Mental Health Specialist in India), Manovaidya incorporates the Neuro-Ayurveda Development System to guide women through a structured assessment and facilitate personalised holistic care by understanding stress, tension and the mind-body connection.\n\nAt Manovaidya, Stress Management & Mind-Body Balance Treatment Focuses On:\n\nChronic Stress\n Mental Stress\n Emotional Stress\n Physical Tension\n Stress-Related Fatigue\n Emotional Exhaustion\n Mind-Body Balance\n Women's Emotional Wellbeing\n\nBook a Structured Stress Management Assessment\n\nIf the stress has become a constant, is caused by emotional, physical tension or lack of sleep, and it's beginning to impact emotional wellbeing, work or relationships, it's time to find out what is causing it.\n\nUnderstanding Stress & Tension\n\nUnderstanding Stress Beyond Symptoms\n\nStress can impact emotional health, physical health, sleep, energy, concentration and quality of life. [1][3]\n\nChronic Stress\n Mental Stress\n Emotional Stress\n Physical Tension\n Stress-Related Fatigue\n\nStress is a normal reaction to situations that are perceived as demanding, uncertain and challenging. [1]\n\nTension is the physical or emotional tension that can occur as a result of prolonged stress.\n\nStress isn't always a bad thing. Stress may help people to respond in an appropriate way during critical events. But if stress isn't relieved and happens regularly or constantly, it can start to impact emotional health and physical health. [1][3]\n\nWomen who are looking to find Stress Management in India or Mind-Body Balance or Chronic Stress or Mental Stress or Emotional Stress or Stress Relief for Women are trying to find out why they are constantly tired, overwhelmed and emotionally drained.\n\nKnowing the link between stress and the mind and body is the first step towards enhancing overall wellbeing.\n\nThe Mind-Body Connection\n\nMuscle tension\n Headaches\n Fatigue\n Digestive discomfort\n Sleep problems\n Reduced energy\n\nSimilarly, ongoing physical discomfort may also affect mood, confidence and emotional wellbeing.\n\nSupporting both physical and emotional health helps create healthier mind-body balance.\n\n\nCommon Signs & Symptoms\n\nEmotional Symptoms\n\nAnxiety, over stimulation, irritability, mood swings, emotional fatigue and inability to relax. [1][2]\n\nPhysical Symptoms\n\nHeadaches, neck or shoulder tension, muscle stiffness, fatigue, poor sleep and digestive discomfort. [1][3]\n\nCognitive Symptoms\n\nLack of concentration, mental fatigue, forgetfulness, constant worrying and racing thoughts. [1][2]\n\nBehavioural Changes\n\nSocial withdrawal, lack of a balance between work and life, emotional eating and trouble managing responsibilities.\n\nStress is unique in each woman's life.\n\nEmotional Symptoms\n\nWomen can have:\n\nAnxiety\n Feeling overwhelmed\n Irritability\n Mood changes\n Emotional exhaustion\n Difficulty relaxing\n Feeling mentally drained\n Reduced motivation\n\nPhysical Symptoms\n\nStress can also play a part in: [1][3]\n\nHeadaches\n Neck and/or shoulder tension\n Muscle stiffness\n Fatigue\n Poor sleep\n Digestive discomfort\n Reduced energy\n Appetite changes\n\nIf physical symptoms persist, they should always be examined by a medical professional.\n\nCognitive Symptoms\n\nSome women notice:\n\nDifficulty concentrating\n Mental fatigue\n Forgetfulness\n Constant worrying\n Racing thoughts\n Difficulty making decisions\n\nBehavioural Changes\n\nIn addition to the above, stress can cause:\n\nSocial withdrawal\nPoor work-life balance\nReduced physical activity\nEmotional eating\nPoor sleep habits\nDifficulty managing responsibilities\n\nWhat is the cause of Chronic Stress?\n\nWork & Family Pressure\n\nChronic stress can be caused by work stress, parenting stress, care giving, family responsibilities and financial stress. [1]\n\nHealth & Hormonal Factors\n\nStress can be affected by health issues, hormonal changes, pregnancy, motherhood and lack of sufficient sleep.\n\nLifestyle & Emotional Load\n\nEvents such as major life changes, relationship difficulties or less time for recovery can impact the mind-body balance.\n\nStress can build up due to several factors that interact with each other.\n\nCommon contributors include:\n\nWorkplace pressure\n Family responsibilities\n Financial concerns\n Relationship challenges\n Parenting stress\n Pregnancy or motherhood\n Hormonal changes\n Health concerns\n Major life transitions\nLack of adequate rest\n\nWhy Early Stress Management Matters\n\n\nAwareness of stress at an early stage enables women to build emotional resilience, bolster coping mechanisms and minimise the effects of stress on their emotional and physical health.\n\nWomen come to expect to feel tired all the time and think of it as a normal life situation.\n\nEarly detection of stress can help women build emotional resistance, develop coping mechanisms, and minimize how much the stress affects them emotionally and physically before it becomes a serious problem.\n\n\nHow Chronic Stress Can Affect Daily Life\n\nStress has a much greater impact than feelings. Stress that persists over a long period of time can eventually have an impact on body health, sleep, relationships, work productivity and overall well-being. [1][3]\n\nDespite being physically and mentally exhausted, many women go on with their daily activities. These changes can be subtle and may not be aware of how much it is impacting their wellbeing in a negative way.\n\nIf women recognise these patterns early they can take steps to achieve better emotional balance and stress management.\n\nEmotional Wellbeing\n\nStress can affect emotional health in a number of ways.\n\nSome women may have these:\n\nFeeling emotionally overwhelmed\n Irritability\n Anxiety\n Mood changes\n Emotional exhaustion\n Reduced patience\n Difficulty relaxing\n Feeling mentally drained\n\nIf the emotional changes persist for an extended time or they affect your day-to-day functioning, professional advice might be helpful.\n\nPhysical Health\n\nStress is not something that just happens in the mind, it is also a stress in the body. [3]\n\nSome females might experience:\n\nFrequent headaches\n Muscle tension\n Neck or shoulder pain\n Fatigue\n Digestive discomfort\n Changes in appetite\n Reduced energy\n Increased physical tiredness\n\nIf there is any doubt that the physical symptoms are persisting, they should always be evaluated by a qualified health care provider to rule out any other medical issues.\n\nSleep\n\nGood sleep and well-being are interrelated.\n\nIf women are stressed and experiencing chronic stress they may notice: [1]\n\nDifficulty falling asleep\nWaking up a lot at night\nRestless sleep\nGetting tired in the morning\nHaving trouble falling asleep at night.\n\nStress can be a vicious cycle, as poor sleep can raise stress further.\n\nWork Performance\n\nA prolonged stress can slowly impact work.\n\nSome women experience:\n\nDifficulty concentrating\n Reduced productivity\n Mental fatigue\n Forgetfulness\n Trouble making decisions\nFeeling overwhelmed by routine tasks\n\nEnsuring emotional wellbeing can contribute to focus and the ability to cope during stressful times.\n\nRelationships & Family Life\n\nStress can also impact communication skills and relationships.\n\nSome women notice:\n\nReduced patience\n Increased misunderstandings\n Emotional withdrawal\n Less time with family and friends\n Struggling to manage family and work duties\n Feeling emotionally disconnected\n\nIt's crucial to maintain healthy communication and emotional support during the stress.\n\nStress vs Anxiety\n\nStress\n\nAssociates with a specific problem\n Improves in response to changes in situation, often\n May express a willingness to consider alternative ways to cope with stress\n\nAnxiety\n\nMay have an exaggerated concern or worry\n Can maintain a behaviour without an external stimulus being present\n Needs professional evaluation for continued\n\nAlthough stress and anxiety are related, they are not exactly the same. [2][4]\n\nStress\n\nStress tends to occur in reaction to a perceived threat or challenge, as might be a work deadline, family priorities or financial concerns. [2]\n\nOften, stress gets better when the stressor changes or coping is better. [2]\n\nAnxiety\n\nAnxiety can manifest as unrealistic worry and fear, which can last for an extended period of time even in the absence of any external threat. [2][4]\nAnxiety disorders are identified mental health disorders that need professional evaluation and treatment. [4]\n\nAwareness of this difference can help women identify if they need more support.\n\nThe Mind-Body Connection\n\nThe brain and body always communicate.\n\nPhysical symptoms may result from emotional stress. Similarly, a persistent physical pain or disease could affect emotional health. [1][3]\n\nThis is a bi-directional connection, therefore physical and emotional health are both important for overall health and wellness.\n\nThe Neuro-Ayurveda Development System\n\nBrain Nourishment System\n Gut Response System\n Neural Network System\n Sensory Integration System\n Behaviour Guidance System\n\nAt Manovaidya, stress management is understood through a holistic perspective.\n\nThe Neuro-Ayurveda Development System takes into account the relationship between the health of the brain, emotional well-being, lifestyle habits, sleep, nutrition, and behaviour, not just the symptoms.\n\nBrain Nourishment System\n\nA healthy brain function is essential for:\n\nEmotional regulation\n Mental clarity\n Focus\n Decision-making\n Memory\n Stress resilience\n\nGood brain health leads to better emotional balance.\n\nGut Response System\n\nThe link between gut and emotional wellbeing is an area of ongoing research. [5]\n\nStressful periods may be managed with support for balanced nutrition and healthy digestion to support energy levels, mood and overall resilience.\n\nNeural Network System\n\nCoping skills are learned and experienced behaviors which become healthy when learned.\n\nMindfulness, Journaling, counselling, reading and learning new coping skills can help build emotional resilience over time. [6]\n\nSensory Integration System\n\nPositive sensory experiences may help promote relaxation.\n\nSome useful activities are:\n\nYoga\n Meditation\n Deep breathing\n Time in nature is therapeutic and healing.\n Listening to relaxing music\n Creative hobbies\n\n\nBehaviour Guidance System\n\nLong-term stress can have an impact on behaviour.\n\nSome women may turn into:\n\nMore emotionally reactive\n Highly self-critical\n Easily frustrated\n Less physically active\n Socially withdrawn\n More likely to use unhealthy coping skills\n\nIdentifying these behaviors early could support women in establishing healthier habits and coping mechanisms.\n\nSupporting Mind-Body Balance\n\nPrioritise Quality Sleep\n Stay Physically Active\n Practice Relaxation Techniques\n Maintain Healthy Nutrition\n Create a Support System\n Seek Professional Guidance\n\nGood day-to-day practices can complement medical or psychological treatment for emotional health. [1][6]\n\nPrioritise Quality Sleep\n\nGood sleep will provide emotional strength, focus and physical healing. [1]\n\nStay Physically Active\n\nAny exercise program that is recommended by your health care provider can help with physical and emotional health. [6]\n\nPractice Relaxation Techniques\n\nRelaxation techniques, such as deep breathing, mindfulness, yoga and meditation, can help to relieve stress. [6][7]\n\nMaintain Healthy Nutrition\n\nEating a balanced diet helps to maintain general good health and emotional well-being.\nBuild a Support System\n\nDiscussing stress and isolation with trusted family members, friends or support groups can help decrease stress and feelings of isolation.\n\nSeek Professional Guidance\n\nIf stress or emotional fatigue, or physical symptoms persist in the workplace, with your relationship, in sleep or in your life, despite engaged in self-care, professional guidance may be helpful in discovering factors and finding personalized coping strategies for stress. If stress is associated with thoughts of self-harm, suicide or inability to keep oneself safe, seek immediate medical/mental health support. [1][2]\n\nHow Manovaidya Supports Women\n\nStep 1: Comprehensive Assessment\nStep 2: Personalized Guidance\nStress & Mind-Body Guidance is Step 3.Step 3: Stress & Mind-Body Guidance\nStep 4: Neuro-Ayurveda System\nStep 5: Ongoing Support\n\nAt Manovaidya, support starts with the person as a whole and not just the symptoms of stress.\nWe have a structured process that involves:\n\n\nStep 1: Comprehensive Assessment\n\nKnowledge of emotional wellbeing, stress, lifestyle, sleep quality, physical health, work-life balance and personal challenges.\n\nStep 2: Personalized Guidance\n\nIndividualized recommendations for each woman based on her emotional, psychological and lifestyle needs.\n\nStep 3: Stress & Mind-Body Wellbeing Guidance\n\nEducating women on the impact of stress on emotional and physical health, and promoting healthy coping.\n\nSteps 4: Neuro-Ayurveda Development System\n\nTaking a five pillars approach to understanding emotional wellbeing, brain health, behaviour and overall wellness.\n\nStep 5: Progress Tracking & Ongoing Support\n\nStructured follow-up to support women to manage their stress over time and to reinforce healthy habits and build emotional resilience.\n\nWhen Should You Consider Professional Guidance?\n\nConsider professional advice if you:\n\nOften find yourself feeling stressed or drained emotionally.\nPay attention to stress that impacts on sleep and/or physical health.\nHave recurring headaches or muscle tension, fatigue due to stress.\nIt's hard to take it easy even while you're on vacation.\nNotice stress that impacts work, or relationships.\nAre emotionally overwhelmed most days.\nContinue experiencing stress despite lifestyle changes.\n\nPersonalized guidance and assessment early can help recognize factors and promote better stress management. [1][2]\n\nWe aim to empower women to gain a deeper understanding of the mind and body connection, build emotional strength, and cultivate the ability to manage stress in the long term with compassion and personalisation.\n\nWhy Women Across India Choose Manovaidya\n\nComprehensive Women's Mental Health Assessment\nNeuro-Ayurveda Development System\nPersonalized Guidance\nHolistic Mind-Body Wellbeing\nFocus on Emotional Resilience & Long-Term Stress Management\nOngoing Support & Progress Tracking\n\n\nStress is a normal part of life, but constantly stressed should not be normal. Emotional load is invisible yet prevalent for many women as they juggle work, family, relationships, parenting and personal expectations. This sustained stress can over time start to impact emotional and physical health. [1][3]\n\nWe know that at Manovaidya stress is not a one cause one effect phenomenon. Brain health, sleep, nutrition, lifestyle habits, hormonal shifts, emotional responses and emotional resilience all have a role to play in emotional wellbeing.\n\nWe do not just want to know what is causing stress for each woman, we want to grasp the full picture of what is happening.\n\nBy providing a safe, supportive setting and a tailored approach, we guide women in developing their emotional strength and long-term well-being.\n\nComprehensive Women's Mental Health Assessment\n\nStress is unique to each woman\n\n\nSome women are interested in getting support for:\n\nChronic stress\n Mental stress\n Emotional exhaustion\n Physical tension\n Poor sleep\n Work-life imbalance\n Stress-related fatigue\n Having trouble managing with daily tasks\n\nThrough our structured assessment, we are helping us to learn about:\n\nEmotional wellbeing\n Stress levels\n Lifestyle habits\n Sleep quality\n Physical health\n Work-life balance\n Family responsibilities\n Personal challenges\n Emotional resilience\n\nBefore developing individually tailored guidance.\n\nNeuro-Ayurveda Development System\n\nManovaidya works within the Neuro-Ayurveda Development System which recognizes emotional wellness in terms of five intersecting systems.\n\nBrain Nourishment System\n Gut Response System\n Neural Network System\n Sensory Integration System\n Behaviour Guidance System\n\nAn understanding of the potential effect of biological, emotional and lifestyle factors on stress, emotional balance and wellbeing can be supported by this framework.\n\nPersonalized Guidance\n\nStress is different for every woman.\nRecommendations are personalised according to:\n\nEmotional concerns\n Lifestyle habits\n Sleep quality\n Physical health\n Work-life balance\n Stress triggers\n Family responsibilities\n Personal goals\n Daily challenges\n\nThis individualised strategy involves providing guidance to each woman specifically based on her emotional and physical requirements.\n\nHolistic Mind-Body Wellbeing\n\nStress has an impact on the emotional and physical health. [1][3]\nIn our approach we take into account the link between the following:\n\nBrain health\n Emotional wellbeing\n Sleep quality\n Nutrition\n Physical activity\n Lifestyle habits\n Stress management\n Healthy relationships\n\nWork on these interrelated areas helps to achieve health and resilience in the long-term while maintaining a healthier mind-body balance.\n\nFocus on Emotional Resilience & Long-Term Stress Management\n\nIt's our job to take stress away for now and for good.\n\nTo support women we want to:\n\nBetter understand their stress patterns \n Build emotional resilience\n Learn positive strategies for coping with stress.\n Improve sleep quality\n Support physical wellbeing\n Improve overall quality of life\n\nThe daily routines that lead to a healthy life are essential to healthy emotional wellbeing.\n\nOngoing Support & Progress Tracking\n\nStress levels and situations vary over time.\n\nThrough regular follow up and structured progress monitoring, women become aware of their progress, consolidate healthy behaviors and continue to build emotional resilience.\n\nWhy Women Across India Trust Manovaidya\n\nWomen from Delhi, Noida, Gurgaon, Faridabad, Ghaziabad and all over India reach out to Manovaidya to avail Stress Management and Mind-Body Balance Support, Women's Mental Health Guidance & Emotional Wellbeing Support online and offline.\n\nFrom chronic stress to physical tension, emotional exhaustion to sleep issues or a lack of work/life balance, we tailor our approach to your specific needs and concerns and place a priority on maintaining your emotional wellbeing in the long run.\n\n\nAbout\n\n\nDr. Ankush Garg\n\n\nAutism, ADHD, Child Development & Mental Health Specialist in India\n\n\nDr. Ankush Garg is the Founder of Manovaidya and the Developer of the Neuro-Ayurveda Development System.\n\n\nHis work extends across Child Development, Adult Mental Health, Women's Mental Health and Senior Mental Health, with a focus on understanding the relationship between brain health, emotional wellbeing and overall quality of life.\n\nMany women seek guidance when chronic stress, emotional exhaustion or mind-body imbalance begin affecting sleep, work, relationships or daily functioning. Through structured assessment, personalised guidance, lifestyle recommendations and continuous follow-up, Dr. Ankush Garg helps women better understand these challenges while supporting long-term emotional wellbeing through the Neuro-Ayurveda Development System.\n\n\nBased in Delhi NCR, Dr. Ankush Garg supports women across India through structured assessment, personalised guidance and holistic mental health care.\n\n\n\nFrequently Asked Questions About Stress Management & Mind-Body Balance\n\n1. What is stress?\n\nStress is a normal body reaction to a situation that poses a challenge or demands. Stress can be useful when it's short term, helping you deal with an important event, but when it occurs often or for a long time, it can have a negative impact on emotional health, physical health and quality of life. [1][3]\n\n2. How does stress affect the body?\n\nStress can affect numerous parts of the body. During stressful situations some women may have headaches, muscle tension, stomach pain, tiredness, trouble sleeping, changes in appetite and heart rate. Any physical symptoms that last should always be checked by a trained health care professional. [1][3]\n\n3. What is the mind-body connection?\n\nThe mind-body connection is the link between emotional wellbeing and physical health. Physical health issues can impact mood, energy and emotional wellbeing, and other emotional stresses can lead to physical symptoms. [3]\n\n4. What are the common signs of chronic stress?\n\nSome common symptoms are fatigue, inability to relax, irritability, anxiety, poor sleep, headaches, muscle tension, digestive upset, emotional fatigue, difficulty concentrating and feeling overwhelmed. Symptoms are different for different individuals. [1]\n\n5. What is the difference between stress and anxiety?\n\nStress is typically triggered by a specific event or situation, like work stress, financial stress or family stress, and tends to ease when the conditions are different. Anxiety can come in the form of an exaggerated worry or fear that can continue when there is no real urgent threat or stressor present. Anxiety disorders are known mental health disorders and should be assessed by professionals. [2][4]\n\n\n6. Can stress affect sleep?\n\nYes. Perceived stress can cause difficulty getting to sleep and staying asleep or waking feeling exhausted and unrefreshed. Not sleeping well can also aggravate stress levels, which can impact emotional and physical health. [1]\n\n7. Can stress cause physical symptoms even when medical tests are normal?\n\nYes. Chronic stress can lead to physical discomforts like headaches, muscle tension, stomach pains or fatigue, even if there is no serious medical problem. If new, severe or persistent physical signs occur, however, it is important to always seek the evaluation of a\n To exclude medical causes, see a qualified health care practitioner. [1][3]\n\n8. How can I reduce stress naturally?\n\nStress management includes regular exercise, sleeping sufficient hours, eating a healthy, balanced diet, deep breathing exercises, yoga, maintaining healthy relationships and allowing for relaxation. If stress persists or is too much, professional advice can be of assistance. [6][7]\n\n9. Can chronic stress affect emotional wellbeing?\n\nYes. Chronic stress can lead to emotional fatigue, impatience, unease, loss of focus and boredom, loss of interest, and sadness or lack of ability to handle everyday tasks. Recognising and managing emotional issues positively can help promote good emotional health. [1][2]\n\n10. When should I seek professional help for stress?\n\nIf the stress persists for more than a few weeks, or it interferes with sleeping, work, relationships, or physical health, if it is hard to deal with on your own, or if it causes a lot of emotional distress, professional help should be sought. Seeking early help can help to identify contributing factors and help to create coping strategies which are tailored to the individual. [1][2]\n\n11. Can stress affect work performance?\n\nYes. Chronic stress can lead to decreased focus, memory, productivity, decision-making and motivation. Additionally, some women might find themselves becoming emotionally drained or having trouble managing work and family duties. [1]\n\n12. Why is maintaining mind-body balance important?\n\nEmotional and physical wellbeing are co-dependent. A holistic approach that supports healthy sleep, nutrition, activity and emotional wellbeing and stress management, can help increase energy levels, resilience and quality of life. [3][6]\n\n13. How does Manovaidya support women experiencing chronic stress?\n\nSupport at Manovaidya starts with the individual, not just the symptoms of stress. We provide structured assessment, personalised guidance, and the Neuro-Ayurveda Development System, empowering women to grasp the importance of emotional wellness, lifestyle practices, sleep, behaviour and the mind-body connection. We aim to help with long term emotional wellbeing in a whole-personliised way.\n\n14. Can stress management improve overall wellbeing?\n\nYes. When stress is controlled through healthy lifestyle habits, evidence-based psychological support, stress management techniques and, when indicated, medical care recommended by qualified health care professionals, many women experience an improvement in emotional resilience, sleep quality, energy level, and functioning throughout the day. This is different for different people. [2][6]\n\n15. When should I seek urgent medical or mental health support?\n\nIf you are feeling stressed, or distressed, and you are having thoughts of hurting yourself or killing yourself or if you think you may do something to hurt or kill yourself, seek immediate medical attention. In such cases, no medical or mental health services should be denied in emergencies. [2]\n\nReferences\n[1] World Health Organization (WHO) – Stress\n Stress ki definition, short-term vs excessive stress, concentration, sleep, headaches, physical discomfort aur emotional effects ke liye strong primary reference.\n https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/stress/\n[2] National Institute of Mental Health (NIMH) – I’m So Stressed Out! Stress vs Anxiety\n Stress vs anxiety, excessive worry, sleep problems, tension, headaches aur professional support ki need wale sections ke liye highly relevant.\n https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/so-stressed-out-fact-sheet\n[3] American Psychological Association (APA) – Stress Effects on the Body\n Mind-body connection, muscle tension, gastrointestinal effects aur chronic stress ke physical impact ko support karta hai.\n https://www.apa.org/topics/stress/body\n[4] National Institute of Mental Health (NIMH) – Anxiety Disorders\n Persistent anxiety, excessive worry aur normal anxiety se anxiety disorder ko distinguish karne wale portions ke liye relevant.\n https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/topics/anxiety-disorders\n[5] National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NCCIH) – Stress\n Mindfulness, meditation aur stress-management approaches ke scientific evidence ke liye useful.\n https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/stress\n[6] NCCIH – Mind and Body Approaches for Stress and Anxiety\n Yoga, mindfulness, meditation aur mind-body interventions ke stress/anxiety evidence ko support karta hai.\n https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/providers/digest/mind-and-body-approaches-for-stress-science\n[7] Chandrasekhar K, Kapoor J, Anishetty S. – Ashwagandha for Stress and Anxiety: Randomized Double-Blind Placebo-Controlled Study\n Ayurvedic perspective mein Ashwagandha aur stress par clinical evidence dene ke liye relevant research paper. Isko general Ayurveda evidence ki tarah cite karein, Manovaidya ke proprietary system ke evidence ke roop mein nahi.\n https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23439798/\n[8] Ministry of AYUSH, Government of India – Ayurveda\n Ayurveda-based holistic health framework ke background/reference ke liye official Indian government source.\n https://ayush.gov.in/\n[9] Central Council for Research in Ayurvedic Sciences (CCRAS)\n Ayurveda research aur evidence-based Ayurvedic research context ke liye Government of India ka relevant research body.\n https://ccras.nic.in/\n\n\n\n\n";

